{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And they made the tunics of linen, woven work, for Aaron and for his sons.</b> It appears from here that the tunics were identical for the father and for the sons, even though concerning Aaron it says, \"And you shall weave the tunic of linen\" (Exodus 28:39), and concerning his sons it says simply, \"You shall make tunics\" (ibid. v. 40). For the intention is: you shall make tunics like the one mentioned. And so it is written (ibid. v. 4), \"A breastpiece and an ephod and a robe and a tunic of checkered work, a turban and a sash; and they shall make holy garments for Aaron your brother and for his sons, to minister to Me\" — for \"for Aaron\" and \"for his sons\" refer back to \"a tunic of checkered work, a turban and a sash,\" since they are all identical. And likewise what it says here (Exodus 39:28), \"and the turban of linen and the headdresses of the linen caps,\" includes Aaron and his sons, for the מִצְנֶפֶת and the מִגְבָּעוֹת are one in their making, except that Aaron winds it (מְצַנֵּף) and his sons fit them on their heads (מְחַבְּשִׁים), as I explained (above 28:31, 37)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {0 "The verse reference לעיל כח לא, לז (above 28:31, 37) appears to point to Ramban's earlier comment on the priestly garments; the citation as written may contain a copyist's inconsistency in verse numbering."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:19:44.749616086Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}